<!-- ╔═══ FAMILY A ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
     One analogy per realm. The reader knows every part already, so the
     only new thing to learn is the wiring — which is also the product.
     ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝ -->
<div class="familybar">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="fam">Family A · The analogy stack</span>
    <span class="sub">Borrow a known name per realm. The connection is the new part.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ─── A1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">A1</span>
    <span class="argues">The straight version — a row per realm, each anchored, each with its twist.</span>
    <span class="risk">Densest but most informative. Risk: eight rows is a lot for a first screen.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="a1">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2 wide-left">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">A fullstack framework · alpha</p>
        <h1>You already know every part. <span class="but">The wiring is the new part.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Nothing here asks you to learn a new paradigm. Each realm is shaped like
          something you have already used — and all of them read the same
          <code>db/schema.lite</code>, so a field you add shows up in the migration,
          the API's validation and the browser's form rules without being written
          three times.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#code">See the code</a>
          <span class="badge warning" title="Working, but APIs may still move">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="stackrows">
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">Data</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Prisma</b> and <b>ZenStack</b> had a baby
            <span class="twist">Schema DSL, migrations, typed client — with the access rules
            declared in the model, on a SQLite file you own</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">API</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Feathers</b>, with <b>Laravel's</b> batteries
            <span class="twist">Services and a four-phase hook pipeline; queue, cron, mail,
            cache, webhooks — and the CRUD derived rather than written</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">UI</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Svelte</b> + <b>SvelteKit</b> + <b>TanStack Query</b>
            <span class="twist">A compiler and signals, file-tree routing, resource binding —
            except the resource already knows the field rules and the permissions</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">Static</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Astro</b>
            <span class="twist">Prerendered HTML, islands hydrated on visible, idle or media —
            from the same components as the app</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">Jobs</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Sidekiq</b> or <b>BullMQ</b>
            <span class="twist">Queue and cron as <code>app.jobs</code> — on SQLite, so no Redis
            to run, back up or pay for</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">Email</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>MJML</b>
            <span class="twist">Table-based components that survive Outlook, with a real
            plain-text alternative — written in the same language as your pages</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">Style</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Pico</b> or <b>Bulma</b>
            <span class="twist">Semantic classes and six themes, no build step, no utility
            soup — this page is it</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="srow">
          <span class="realm">Tooling</span>
          <span class="like">Like <b>Artisan</b> or <b>rails</b>
            <span class="twist">One CLI from <code>new</code> to <code>deploy</code>, plus a
            language server for the schema</span></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── A2 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">A2</span>
    <span class="argues">Same idea, playful register — a family tree with named parents.</span>
    <span class="risk">Most shareable, most quotable. Risk: cute can read as unserious.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="a2" style="background:var(--surface-sunken)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>several things at once</b></p>
        <h1>What if they'd all been <span class="but">designed together?</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Every part of this stack has a well-known relative. The difference is that
          they share a parent instead of a package registry: one schema, one hook
          pipeline, one error boundary, one CLI. You are not integrating six good
          libraries. You are using one thing that already agrees with itself.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#packages">The whole map</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="parents">
          <span class="parent">Prisma <small>· schema</small></span>
          <span class="parent">ZenStack <small>· policies</small></span>
          <span class="parent">SQLite <small>· one file</small></span>
          <span class="parent">Feathers <small>· services + hooks</small></span>
          <span class="parent">Laravel <small>· batteries</small></span>
          <span class="parent">Svelte <small>· compiler</small></span>
          <span class="parent">SvelteKit <small>· routing</small></span>
          <span class="parent">TanStack <small>· data binding</small></span>
          <span class="parent">Astro <small>· islands</small></span>
          <span class="parent">Sidekiq <small>· jobs</small></span>
          <span class="parent">MJML <small>· email</small></span>
          <span class="parent">Artisan <small>· CLI</small></span>
        </div>

        <p class="descend">↓</p>

        <div class="child">
          <p class="name">Frontier<span>JS</span></p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:.35rem 0 .8rem">
            One schema seeds all of it. Three realms, one vocabulary, fourteen packages
            that were written to fit.
          </p>
          <ul class="kl keep">
            <li>Write the schema — the API and its rules are derived from it</li>
            <li>Write a six-line service when the derived one isn't enough</li>
            <li>Bind the UI to the same schema, rules and permissions included</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── A3 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">A3</span>
    <span class="argues">Round one's BaaS hero, corrected — the frontend half was missing.</span>
    <span class="risk">Widest audience. Risk: “BaaS” sets an expectation of a dashboard.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="a3">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>Supabase, Firebase, PocketBase</b></p>
        <h1>A backend that derives itself. <span class="quiet">And the frontend that knows it.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A hosted platform gives you the first half: declare your data, get endpoints,
          auth, realtime and types. Then it hands you a client SDK and the browser
          starts again from zero — you re-type the validation, re-decide who may see
          the edit button, and hope the two halves stay in agreement.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
          Here both halves come from the same file. And there is no platform: it is a
          package in your <code>node_modules</code>, MIT, over a SQLite file you own.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#code">See the code</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="display:grid; gap:1rem">
        <div class="card stack">
          <h3>The half you'd get anywhere</h3>
          <ul class="kl keep">
            <li>Schema → REST API, no handler written</li>
            <li>Validation and <code>400</code>s from the field rules</li>
            <li>Row permissions compiled into every query</li>
            <li>Realtime on mutation, migrations, types</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card stack">
          <h3>The half that usually stops at the SDK</h3>
          <ul class="kl keep">
            <li><code>resource.fields</code> — the same rules, client-side</li>
            <li><code>resource.can()</code> — the model's own permissions, for hiding a button</li>
            <li><code>make()</code> — a form the schema describes</li>
            <li>Components that also render to static HTML, email and an extension</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
          Client-side permissions are an affordance, never a boundary — the server
          enforces regardless. That is the point of them living in one place.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ╔═══ FAMILY B ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
     The complete-framework audience. Everything here turns on one fact:
     the completeness does not stop at the network boundary.
     ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝ -->
<div class="familybar">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="fam">Family B · The complete framework</span>
    <span class="sub">Laravel / Rails / Django — but it crosses the boundary they stop at.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ─── B1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">B1</span>
    <span class="argues">The concept map, extended — every row familiar until the last two.</span>
    <span class="risk">Instantly legible to a Laravel dev. Risk: says nothing to anyone else.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="b1" style="background:var(--surface-sunken)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>Laravel, Rails, Django</b></p>
        <h1>That kind of complete. <span class="but">In TypeScript. Past the boundary.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A queue, a scheduler, mail, notifications, policies, migrations, generators,
          one CLI — the things that make a framework feel finished rather than started.
          The difference is where they stop. In all three the model layer ends at the
          network boundary, and the browser starts again from nothing.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
          Here the schema keeps going: the form knows the field rules and the button
          knows the permission, because both read the file the migration came from.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#packages">The packages</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card stack">
        <div class="maprows">
          <div class="maprow head"><span>You know</span><span></span><span>Here</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Artisan · rails</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">fli</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Eloquent · ActiveRecord</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">litestone</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Migrations</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">diffed from schema.lite</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Policies · Pundit</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">@@gate · @@allow</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Form requests · validation</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">derived from the fields</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Resource controllers</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">createService(&#123; model &#125;)</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Middleware</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">hooks · around/before/after/error</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Queues · Scheduler</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">app.jobs</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Mail · Notifications</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">app.notify</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Broadcasting</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">app.channel</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Blade · ERB · Jinja</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">.mesa</span></div>
          <div class="maprow"><span class="from">Inertia · Hotwire</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">sierra</span></div>
          <div class="maprow none"><span class="from"><em>no equivalent</em> — the validation rules, in the browser</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">resource.fields</span></div>
          <div class="maprow none"><span class="from"><em>no equivalent</em> — the model's permissions, in the browser</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">resource.can()</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin-top:.5rem">
          Everything above the line you can already get. The two below it are the argument.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── B2 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">B2</span>
    <span class="argues">The same claim without naming anyone — the boundary itself is the visual.</span>
    <span class="risk">Works on a reader with no Laravel background. Risk: less immediate hook.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="b2">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Where every other framework stops</p>
        <h1>Full-stack usually means <span class="quiet">two stacks and a fetch.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          The server has a model, validation, policies, a hook pipeline. The browser
          has none of it, so it gets a second implementation of the same three facts —
          in a different language, kept in agreement by hand, and discovered to be
          wrong by a user.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
          FrontierJS puts the same four concepts on both sides of the wire, and the
          same schema under both.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#extend">The four concepts</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="sides">
          <div class="side">
            <h3>API realm · server</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><code>createService(&#123; model &#125;)</code></li>
              <li>hooks — <code>around before after error</code></li>
              <li>validation from the field rules</li>
              <li><code>@@gate</code> · <code>@@allow</code>, compiled into SQL</li>
              <li>the result envelope</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="side">
            <h3>UI realm · browser</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><code>createResource('leads')</code></li>
              <li>hooks — <code>around before after error</code></li>
              <li><code>resource.fields</code> · <code>resource.validate()</code></li>
              <li><code>resource.can()</code> — affordance, not boundary</li>
              <li>the same envelope, unwrapped</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="boundary">↑ one schema under both ↑</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── B3 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">B3</span>
    <span class="argues">Convention over configuration, extended to the whole app layout.</span>
    <span class="risk">Strong for the Rails crowd. Risk: layout is table stakes, not a differentiator.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="b3" style="background:var(--surface-sunken)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>Rails' conventions</b></p>
        <h1>Convention over configuration. <span class="but">Including the browser.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          One layout for the whole application: a directory per realm, the schema above
          all of them, and the same six folders inside each. You do not decide where
          config goes, where tests go, or how the frontend finds the API — and because
          the convention is shared, a generator can write into all three at once.
        </p>
        <ul class="kl keep" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          <li><code>fli make:resource lead</code> writes the service <em>and</em> the route</li>
          <li><code>model Lead</code> → service <code>leads</code> → <code>db.lead</code>, checked in three places</li>
          <li><code>fli project:map</code> prints what exists and what derives from what</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#code">See the code</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.S0}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ╔═══ FAMILY C ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
     The reader's own package.json. Three framings of the same integration
     tax: dependency count, vocabulary collision, and rule duplication.
     ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝ -->
<div class="familybar">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="fam">Family C · The assembled stack</span>
    <span class="sub">Your own package.json — the pain is supplied by the reader.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ─── C1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">C1</span>
    <span class="argues">The dependency diff — fourteen good libraries that never met.</span>
    <span class="risk">Most concrete. Risk: “fewer deps” is a weak claim; the copy must fix that.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="c1">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>the stack you already assembled</b></p>
        <h1>The same stack. <span class="but">Designed together.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Nothing on the left is a bad choice. They are all good — they just never met.
          Each brings a config file, an error shape, a notion of a user and its own idea
          of what your data looks like, and holding them in agreement is the job that
          never appears on a ticket.
        </p>
        <ul class="kl keep" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          <li>One schema — the ORM, the API validation and the form rules read one file</li>
          <li>One hook pipeline — the same four phases in both realms</li>
          <li>One error boundary — every package's errors become a status the same way</li>
          <li>One CLI — scaffold, migrate, run, build, deploy</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#idea">Why it works this way</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="diff">
        <div>
          <h3>Today</h3>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.S1}</pre>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Instead</h3>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.S2}</pre>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin-top:.75rem">
            Fewer dependencies is not the point. <strong>One vocabulary</strong> is the
            point — the count is just what that looks like from outside.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── C2 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">C2</span>
    <span class="argues">The tax restated as vocabulary — the same fact spelled four ways.</span>
    <span class="risk">The truest description of the pain. Risk: needs a reader who's felt it.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="c2" style="background:var(--surface-sunken)">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>every stack you've integrated</b></p>
        <h1>Four libraries. <span class="quiet">Four words for the same thing.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Integration cost is not the install. It is that each library names the same
          fact differently, so you spend your afternoons translating — and every
          translation is a place the two can disagree without anything failing loudly.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
          One vocabulary is not a style preference. It is the difference between one
          fact and four copies of it.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#extend">The vocabulary</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="vocab">
        <div class="vrow">
          <p class="q">Who is asking?</p>
          <div class="spellings"><span>req.user</span><span>ctx.auth</span><span>session.user</span><span>auth()</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="vrow">
          <p class="q">What does this record look like?</p>
          <div class="spellings"><span>schema.prisma</span><span>z.object()</span><span>openapi.yaml</span><span>useForm rules</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="vrow">
          <p class="q">Who may read it?</p>
          <div class="spellings"><span>RLS policy</span><span>middleware</span><span>ability rules</span><span>hidden button</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="vrow">
          <p class="q">What went wrong?</p>
          <div class="spellings"><span>PrismaError</span><span>ZodError</span><span>HttpException</span><span>&#123; error: true &#125;</span></div>
        </div>

        <p class="descend" style="margin:.3rem 0">↓</p>

        <div class="vrow one">
          <p class="q" style="color:var(--ink)">All four, once</p>
          <div class="spellings">
            <span>auth()</span><span>model Lead</span><span>@@allow</span><span>toFrameworkError()</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── C3 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="marker">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="tag">C3</span>
    <span class="argues">One business rule, written three times — then once. The sharpest of the three.</span>
    <span class="risk">Strongest single argument on the page. Risk: it is a detail, not a category.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="heroblock" id="c3">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="anchor">Closest thing · <b>how you do it today</b></p>
        <h1>One rule. <span class="quiet">Three places.</span> <span class="but">Until one of them forgets.</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          “Only the owner can read a lead” is one sentence, and most stacks make you say
          it in three languages: once in the database, once in the handler, once in the
          component that hides the row. Three chances to be right, and no error when
          they stop matching.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
          Declare it on the Model and it compiles into every query, under every caller —
          including the ones written after you left.
        </p>
        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#idea">How it compiles</a>
          <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="thrice">
        <div>
          <p class="where">Today · database</p>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.S3}</pre>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="where">Today · API</p>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.S4}</pre>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="where">Today · UI</p>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.S5}</pre>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="where" style="color:var(--color-primary)">Here · once, on the Model</p>
          <pre class="code">{@html sample.S6}</pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- The page's own closing note. The site footer is the layout's. -->
<div class="container pagenote">
<span>Nine candidates across three anchor families. Not a page to ship as-is.</span>
    <span>Styled entirely by <code>@frontierjs/css</code>. <a href="/index2/">Round one →</a> · <a href="/">Current home page →</a></span>
</div>

<style>
/* ── review scaffolding ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .familybar { background: var(--ink); color: var(--surface); padding: 1.5rem 0; }
  .familybar .container { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .familybar .fam { font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .familybar .sub { opacity: .72; font-size: .88rem; }

  .marker { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--surface-sunken); padding: .8rem 0; font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .marker .container { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .marker .tag { font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-primary); font-size: .72rem; }
  .marker .argues { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .marker .risk { margin-left: auto; }

  /* ── hero frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .heroblock { min-height: 86vh; display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 4rem 0; scroll-margin-top: 3.5rem; }

  .anchor { display: inline-flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
  .anchor b { color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 600; }

  h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem); line-height: 1.05; letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .4em 0 .35em; max-width: 22ch; }
  h1 .but { color: var(--color-primary); }
  h1 .quiet { color: var(--ink-mute); }
  h3 { font-size: .95rem; margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.06rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 56ch; line-height: 1.6; }


  .split2 { display: grid; gap: 2.5rem; grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 62rem) { .split2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .split2.wide-left { grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); }
  @media (min-width: 62rem) { .split2.wide-left { grid-template-columns: 1.15fr .85fr; } }

  /* Untoned .btn.outlined paints --color-primary on --surface — below AA in
     two shipped themes. Take the neutral ink, as .btn.ghost already does. */
  .btn.outlined { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--rule-strong); }

  /* ── A1: the analogy stack ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .stackrows { display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .srow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 5.5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; padding: .72rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); align-items: baseline; }
  .srow:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .srow .realm { font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .srow .like { font-size: 1rem; }
  .srow .like b { font-weight: 600; }
  .srow .twist { display: block; font-size: .83rem; color: var(--ink-mute); margin-top: .12rem; }
  .srow .twist code { font-size: .78rem; }

  /* ── A2: family tree ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .parents { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4rem; }
  .parent { border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 999px; padding: .3rem .8rem; font-size: .86rem; background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .parent small { color: var(--ink-mute); font-size: .74rem; }
  .descend { text-align: center; color: var(--rule-strong); font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 1; margin: .9rem 0; }
  .child { border: 1px solid var(--color-primary); border-radius: 12px; padding: 1rem 1.15rem; background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .child .name { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  .child .name span { color: var(--color-primary); }

  /* ── shared: keep/lose ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .kl { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .45rem; font-size: .89rem; }
  .kl li { display: flex; gap: .6rem; align-items: flex-start; }
  .kl li::before { flex: none; font-weight: 700; }
  .keep li::before { content: "✓"; color: var(--color-success); }
  .lose li::before { content: "✕"; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .lose li { color: var(--ink-mute); }

  /* ── B: mapping table ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .maprows { display: grid; gap: 0; font-size: .86rem; }
  .maprow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr; gap: .7rem; align-items: baseline; padding: .38rem 0; border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--rule); }
  .maprow:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .maprow .from { color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .maprow .to { font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .8rem; }
  .maprow .arrow { color: var(--rule-strong); }
  .maprow.none { padding: .5rem 0; }
  .maprow.none .from { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .maprow.none .to { color: var(--color-primary); font-family: inherit; font-size: .86rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .maprow.head { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-bottom: .3rem; }
  .maprow.head span { font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); }

  /* ── B2: two sides of the boundary ─────────────────────────────────── */
  .sides { display: grid; gap: 0; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .sides { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .side { padding: 1.05rem 1.15rem; background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .side + .side { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .side + .side { border-top: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule); } }
  .side h3 { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .side ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .38rem; font-size: .85rem; }
  .side code { font-size: .78rem; }
  .boundary { text-align: center; font-size: .74rem; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); padding: .7rem 0; }

  /* ── C: diffs and vocabularies ─────────────────────────────────────── */
  .diff { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) { .diff { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .diff h3 { font-size: .8rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); }

  .vocab { display: grid; gap: .55rem; }
  .vrow { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: .7rem .9rem; background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .vrow .q { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .vrow .spellings { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .3rem; font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .76rem; }
  .vrow .spellings span { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: .15rem .45rem; color: var(--ink-mute); background: var(--surface); }
  .vrow.one { border-color: var(--color-primary); }
  .vrow.one .spellings span { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--rule-strong); }

  .thrice { display: grid; gap: .6rem; }
  .thrice .where { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 .25rem; }
  .navset .sep { color: var(--rule-strong); padding: 0 .2rem; font-size: .8rem; }
</style>
